nect to 172.17.0.1 port 36849 after 135161 ms: Couldn't connect to server\n223a730 Outline rev. 10: branches re-recorded on the primary common-target-set results (Brief rev. 10)\n49865a2 Outline rev. 9: G2 met, C7 as second matrix with confirmatory role; sentences 4-5 aligned with Brief rev. 9\n7d35817 Outline rev. 8: Brief rev. 9 (residual wording, better-inference as hypothesis tied to Table 3)\n3:*Rev. 10, by story-repo (see the revision log). Built from the `Brief` (idea-repo, rev. 10) and the pre-registration (`experiment-repo` `prereg/PREREG.md`, commit 7731e49) and the objective (`story-repo`#1). It says what to test and which figure carries which claim; it never says what a result must be. Each claim has a branch for the case it fails — a failing claim is reported as a finding, not repaired.*\n14:5. Main result (numbers from the common-target-set rerun): the error-vs-k curve on held-out groups; the k that gets within ε points of the floor, for a range of ε; designed selection matters when k is very small (≤ 5) and random-k is within noise from about k = 8 at the coarse scale; beyond that the error sits in the floor, which is larger than the factor model predicts; that better inference rather than better selection would lower it is a hypothesis, carried as a claim only if Table 3 shows an inference rule lowering the excess over the floor by more than δ_floor (part of the residual may be the floor itself), and no k resolves neighbouring models (floor ≈ 5 points on the common target set; the pre-set margin of about 0.1 point, set by adjacent-model gaps, is not reached at any k).\n17:The paper is **evidence-led**: the real-data curve is the headline (Fig. 1), theory explains it, the scope map bounds it. Theory is not the first result on the page, because the data decide whether it is the explanation or only a reference.\n19:## 2. Section plan (what each part says; page split is the venue's call)\n22:| 1 Introduction | evaluator's situation, the question Q, the headline (one sentence with numbers from the final run), contributions list (the three: empirical answer under matching protocol; k*(ε) with misfit term; scope map), explicit \"not claimed: new selection algorithm\" | Fig. 1 on page 1–2 |\n24:| 3 Problem and protocol | setting, data matrix definition (one benchmark = one task, rules fixed beforehand), group-wise hold-out, temporal/strength split, **common-target-set evaluation (below)**, k counted inside the model's own run set, metrics, floors, pre-set margins δ/Δ and their aggregation, one primary contrast | Table 1 (coverage filter), protocol figure/schematic if the budget allows |\n25:| 4 Method | factor model with missing data (EM), posterior of the new model's latent vector given run set, selection objective F(S), greedy, cost-aware variant (only if a real cost source exists), the inference rules and selection rules compared; **conditions of applicability and cost** (data requirements: minimum M, coverage, rank; compute: EM fit and greedy time per held-out model on the 4-CPU machine; evaluator's cost: k/N of the benchmarks, in MTEB `evaluation_time` only if that source is confirmed) | baselines table; small conditions-and-cost table (main text if space, else appendix, with one sentence in §4 and §7) |\n27:| 6 Experiments | 6.1 structure/rank (H1); 6.2 error vs k on real held-out groups (headline); 6.3 designed vs random-k (H2) with the negative branch; 6.4 theory vs measurement, synthetic ladder + real misfit (H3); 6.5 adaptivity (H4); 6.6 scope conditions (H5) | Figs. 1–5, Tables 2–3 |\n28:| 7 Discussion and limits | what k an evaluator should use and with which caveats; MNAR run sets, single measurements per cell, embedding ≠ generative LLMs, group-count limits, where theory failed | |\n33:- per held-out model (group-wise), the benchmarks the model has are split into a **candidate pool C** (selectable) and a **target set T*\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "7e42d7aa-5d4b-5a24-86fd-7b1573